- How would you optimize to O(nk) space?
- How would you reconstruct the exact substrings efficiently without extra passes?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests modeling of selecting multiple disjoint subarrays with a fixed cost and a cap on count, including interval reconstruction. It blends standard DP on subarrays with a knapsack-like limit and requires careful state design.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix index i and subarray count t
- Prefix sums for fast subarray sums
- Transitions: skip vs end-subarray
- Reconstruction via back-pointers; space/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for (let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      par[i][t] = {type:'skip'};
      if (t>0){
        for (let L=1; L<=i; L++){
          const prev = dp[i-L][t-1];
          if (prev <= -Infinity/2) continue;
          const cand = prev + (pref[i]-pref[i-L]) - c;
          if (cand > dp[i][t]){
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            par[i][t] = {type:'end', end:i, len:L};
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would walk back from (n, argmax_t)
  return {maxValue: dp[n].reduce((a,b)=> Math.max(a,b), -Infinity)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time and O(nk) space, and then reduce to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How would you extend for at most k subarrays with variable per-subarray costs or negative values?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over partitioning with a quadratic segment cost, requiring prefix sums, base cases, and reconstruction, plus discussion of optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t] for first i elements into t segments
- Prefix sums for O(1) segment sums
- Quadratic segment cost and optimization (CHT or Divide-and-Conquer)
- Reconstructing segment boundaries with a prev table
- Time/space trade-offs and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k && t <= i; t++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        const diff = S[i] - S[j];
        const cost = diff * diff;
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + cost;
        if (val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return { cost: dp[n][k] };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to structure DP with prefix states, reconstruct decisions, and enforce a gap between segments — a common real-world pattern in budgeting or scheduling.

## Key Concepts
- Kadane variant to track best subarray ending at i
- 2D DP dp[i][t] for first i elements and t blocks
- Reconstructing endpoints with backpointers; gap handling

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const endSum = Array(n).fill(0);
  const endStart = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (i === 0 || endSum[i-1] + A[i] < A[i]) {
      endSum[i] = A[i];
      endStart[i] = i;
    } else {
      endSum[i] = endSum[i-1] + A[i];
      endStart[i] = endStart[i-1];
    }
  }
  // dp and backtracking would follow (omitted for brevity)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you relax the gap constraint or support at-most-k blocks?
- How does the solution change with negative numbers or ties in endpoint choices?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests DP on grids with a minimax objective, reconstruction, and practical edge handling (blocked cells).

## Key Concepts
- DP on 2D grid with a minimax recurrence
- Path reconstruction via predecessor tracking
- Space optimization opportunities (1D row/column DP)
- INF handling for blockers

## Code Example
```javascript
function minMaxRisk(r) {
  const n = r.length, m = r[0].length;
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(m).fill(INF));
  const from = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(m).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = r[0][0];
  for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) { dp[0][j] = Math.max(dp[0][j-1], r[0][j]); from[0][j] = 0; }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) { dp[i][0] = Math.max(dp[i-1][0], r[i][0]); from[i][0] = 1; }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) {
      const up = Math.max(dp[i-1][j], r[i][j]);
      const left = Math.max(dp[i][j-1], r[i][j]);
      if (up <= left) { dp[i][j] = up; from[i][j] = 0; } else { dp[i][j] = left; from[i][j] = 1; }
    }
  }
  return {minMaxRisk: dp[n-1][m-1], parent: from};
}
```

